const body = `<h1>7.3c &#8212; Leaderboard: ArrayList of Objects</h1>

<p>
    <strong>Type:</strong> Expand Application. Strings were easy. Now store
    your own objects in an ArrayList, sort them, and find the top performers.
    The full leaderboard pattern.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Expand Application</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>The Goal</h3>

    <p>Build a <code>Player</code> class with a name and score, store them
    in an ArrayList, sort by score, and print a ranked leaderboard.</p>

    <pre><code><span class="output">=== LEADERBOARD ===
1. Alice      - 2150
2. Diana      - 1880
3. Bob        - 1720
4. Charlie    - 1610
5. Eve        - 1540</span></code></pre>

    <p><strong>Reference:</strong>
    <a href="../docs/unit-07/leaderboard.html">Leaderboard Walkthrough</a>
    &#8212; full reference.</p>

    <h3>Build It: Player Class + Leaderboard</h3>

    <p>Create <code>Player.java</code>:</p>

    <pre><code>public class Player {
    private String name;
    private int score;

    public Player(String name, int score) {
        this.name = name;
        this.score = score;
    }

    public String getName() { return name; }
    public int getScore() { return score; }

    public String toString() {
        return name + " - " + score;
    }
}</code></pre>

    <p>Create <code>Leaderboard.java</code>. Add at least 5 players:</p>

    <pre><code>import java.util.ArrayList;

public class Leaderboard {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ArrayList&lt;Player&gt; players = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();

        players.add(new Player("Alice", 2150));
        players.add(new Player("Bob", 1720));
        players.add(new Player("Charlie", 1610));
        players.add(new Player("Diana", 1880));
        players.add(new Player("Eve", 1540));

        // YOUR CODE: print unsorted
    }
}</code></pre>

    <h3>The Sort Challenge</h3>

    <p>Sort the ArrayList by score, highest first. You CANNOT use
    <code>Collections.sort()</code> yet &#8212; that requires Comparable
    (coming in 7.4). Instead, use the selection sort pattern you
    learned with arrays:</p>

    <pre><code>// Selection sort by score (descending)
for (int i = 0; i < players.size() - 1; i++) {
    int maxIndex = i;
    for (int j = i + 1; j < players.size(); j++) {
        if (players.get(j).getScore() > players.get(maxIndex).getScore()) {
            maxIndex = j;
        }
    }
    // swap players[i] with players[maxIndex]
    Player temp = players.get(i);
    players.set(i, players.get(maxIndex));
    players.set(maxIndex, temp);
}</code></pre>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic Checkpoint</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Why does the inner loop start at <code>i + 1</code>
                instead of 0?</li>
            <li>What changes if you want ascending order (lowest first)?</li>
            <li>How is this sort structurally identical to the card
                shuffle swap pattern? What's the only thing that
                changed?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Extend It (pick one)</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Add the ability to update a player's score and re-sort.</li>
            <li>Track "games played" per player and compute average
                score per game.</li>
            <li>Add new players from Scanner input and insert them
                in the correct sorted position (no full re-sort).</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

